{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2167-02-04 22:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee, one of your hospital doctors. You're here because of shortness of breath and green sputum from your trach. How are you feeling right now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2167-02-04 22:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, Doctor. I’m still short of breath, especially when I walk. My throat and trach hurt, and I’ve been coughing up thick green mucus."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2167-02-04 22:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. You mentioned chills too, but no fever. We checked your vitals and labs in the ER. Your white count is normal, and your chest X-ray is clear. No pneumonia or blood clot."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2167-02-04 22:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not an infection? But it feels just like my past tracheitis episodes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2167-02-04 22:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s possible, but so far, nothing shows an active infection. ENT looked at your airway and saw dry mucus at the carina. They recommend more humidification and saline to loosen it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2167-02-04 22:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. But why am I so short of breath even when resting?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2167-02-04 22:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re looking into that. Your oxygen is 100% on the trach mask, but we noticed you drop a bit when walking. We’ll monitor that and do more tests tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2167-02-05 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. We did a CT scan yesterday to rule out a blood clot. It came back negative. No PE, no aortic issues. Your lungs look clear."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2167-02-05 08:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good to hear. But I still feel lightheaded, especially when I sit up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2167-02-05 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We checked your blood pressure sitting and standing. It drops a little, and you haven’t been eating or drinking much because of throat pain. We’re starting IV fluids to help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2167-02-05 08:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, okay. That might explain it. I haven’t had much down my throat—literally."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2167-02-05 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll keep the fluids going and encourage small sips. We also want to check your heart with an echo to make sure it’s not contributing to your breathing issues."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2167-02-06 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The echo was done yesterday. Image quality was suboptimal, but your heart looks strong. No signs of pulmonary hypertension, though we can’t rule it out completely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2167-02-06 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So my heart’s okay. But why do I keep desatting when I walk?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2167-02-06 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Given your tracheobronchomalacia, it’s likely due to air trapping and mismatch in airflow. We’re calling interventional pulmonology to evaluate further."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2167-02-06 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will they do another scope?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2167-02-06 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, likely a bronchoscopy. ENT already did a laryngoscopy and saw dry mucus. They didn’t see signs of acute infection or granulation tissue."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2167-02-06 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I hope the scope helps. My throat pain is still bad. The oxycodone isn’t touching it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2167-02-06 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re adding liquid acetaminophen for better pain control. We also want to restart your iron pills. Your hemoglobin is low, and you have a history of anemia."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2167-02-06 09:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I stopped the iron because it made me nauseous. Can I take it with food?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2167-02-06 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You can try, but it absorbs better on an empty stomach. We’ll start low and see how you tolerate it. Your PCP will manage it long-term."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2167-02-07 10:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your sputum culture came back. It grew MRSA, but there are no signs of active infection—just colonization. We won’t start antibiotics."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2167-02-07 10:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had MRSA before. Should I be worried?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2167-02-07 10:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not right now. It’s common in patients with tracheostomies. We just need to be cautious with any future infections."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2167-02-07 10:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. Any update on the bronchoscopy?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2167-02-07 10:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Scheduled for tomorrow. We want to check for any airway collapse or blockage that could explain your symptoms."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2167-02-08 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Quick update—your viral panel came back negative. No flu or RSV. So we’re still ruling out infection as the main cause."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2167-02-08 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good. I was worried about catching something in the hospital."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2167-02-08 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We are too. That’s why we’re being careful. Your breathing treatments—albuterol, ipratropium, and saline nebs—are helping keep your airway moist."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2167-02-08 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "They do help. My cough is looser now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2167-02-08 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s great. We’re also giving guaifenesin to thin the mucus. Keep drinking fluids when you can."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2167-02-09 13:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You just got back from bronchoscopy. The trach is in good position. No obstruction. But they saw severe tracheobronchomalacia and a small amount of non-infectious secretions."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2167-02-09 13:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So nothing new? Just my usual malacia?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2167-02-09 13:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. The pulmonologist thinks your hypoxia is chronic, due to V/Q mismatch from the malacia. They suggest home oxygen and follow-up with interventional pulmonology."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2167-02-09 13:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Home oxygen? I’ve never needed that before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2167-02-09 13:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just for now, especially when you’re active. We want to prevent desats. You’ll use a pulse oximeter at home to track your levels."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2167-02-09 13:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I can do that. What about my swallowing? It still feels like food gets stuck."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2167-02-09 13:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve scheduled a video swallow study. It will check for aspiration and how well you’re moving food down. Your laryngeal stenosis could be contributing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2167-02-10 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The swallow study was done. No aspiration seen. Some residue, but stable compared to last time. You’re safe to continue your regular diet."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2167-02-10 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. I was worried I’d need a feeding tube again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2167-02-10 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, you’re doing well. We’ll just keep monitoring. Your throat pain is improving, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2167-02-10 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A little. The new pain med helps. But I still need it pretty often."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2167-02-10 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll give you a short supply of liquid oxycodone for home. Your PCP will handle long-term pain. You might also benefit from a chronic pain clinic referral."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2167-02-10 10:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That might help. This pain is constant."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2167-02-11 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve made good progress. Your oxygen levels are more stable, and you’re walking farther without desatting. We’re planning for discharge in a couple of days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2167-02-11 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. What do I need to do at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2167-02-11 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Continue the saline nebs, stay hydrated, and use the 3% saline nebulizer we’re prescribing. Also, keep using the nasal saline spray."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2167-02-11 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the oxygen?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2167-02-11 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Use it when you’re active or if your pulse ox drops below 92%. Keep a diary of your symptoms and oxygen levels."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2167-02-11 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I need more tests?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2167-02-11 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. Your PCP will arrange a repeat echo in two months to check for pulmonary hypertension. Also, pulmonary function tests with DLCO."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2167-02-11 09:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And follow-up with pulmonology?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2167-02-11 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, and interventional pulmonology. If you remain symptomatic, they might consider stenting to improve airflow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2167-02-12 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Final update: your labs are stable, you’re breathing well on room air, and your pain is manageable. We’re clearing you for discharge tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2167-02-12 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Great. What’s the discharge plan?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2167-02-12 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll go home with oxygen, saline nebs, iron supplements, and pain meds. We’ll send instructions to your PCP."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2167-02-12 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my mental health meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2167-02-12 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll continue fluoxetine. Keep in touch with your therapist. Chronic illness can be tough on mood."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2167-02-12 10:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I appreciate that. I’ve been feeling down lately."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2167-02-12 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll make sure you’re supported. Any other questions before discharge?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 61,
      "time": "2167-02-12 10:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, I think that covers it. Thank you for explaining everything."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 62,
      "time": "2167-02-12 10:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll see you in follow-up. Take care, and call if anything worsens."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 63,
      "time": "2167-02-13 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. You’re officially discharged. Your trach site looks clean, lungs clear after cough. You’re alert and walking independently."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 64,
      "time": "2167-02-13 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel ready to go home. Thanks again for all your help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 65,
      "time": "2167-02-13 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Remember: monitor your oxygen, take your meds, and follow up with your specialists. Call us if you have fever, chest pain, or worsening breathing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 66,
      "time": "2167-02-13 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Have a good day, Doctor."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 67,
      "time": "2167-02-13 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You too. Take care."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2167-02-04 22:19:00",
    "admission_end": "2167-02-13 19:00:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A young woman with a history of tracheobronchomalacia, laryngeal stenosis, and a permanent tracheostomy was admitted for shortness of breath, green sputum, throat pain, and dysphagia. Workup revealed no acute infection, pulmonary embolism, or cardiac dysfunction. Imaging and bronchoscopy showed severe tracheobronchomalacia and dry mucus at the carina, but no obstruction or active infection. She experienced ambulatory hypoxia, likely due to V/Q mismatch from her underlying airway disease. She was treated with nebulized saline, bronchodilators, and aggressive pulmonary toilet. MRSA was cultured but deemed colonizing. Anemia was addressed with ferrous sulfate restart. She improved with supportive care, was discharged on home oxygen, and advised to follow up with pulmonology, interventional pulmonology, and her PCP for ongoing management.",
    "problems": [
      "Unspecified chronic bronchitis",
      "Bronchopulmonary dysplasia originating in the perinatal period",
      "Stenosis of larynx",
      "Tracheostomy status",
      "Congenital tracheomalacia",
      "Congenital bronchomalacia",
      "Dysphagia, unspecified",
      "Carrier or suspected carrier of Methicillin resistant Staphylococcus aureus",
      "Hypoxemia",
      "Dizziness and giddiness",
      "Iron deficiency anemia, unspecified",
      "Major depressive disorder, single episode, unspecified",
      "Gastro-esophageal reflux disease without esophagitis"
    ]
  }
}
